ava: Hi team — customer reporting sudden 429s and long Retry-After during a short spike. They say it started ~2026-11-02T16:23:00Z and lasted ~2 minutes. They’re on hosted API (org: org-987). Could this be burst credits exhaustion?

emma: Sorry to hear that. Can you paste the raw response headers and a sample request id? Also which route (/v1/completions, /v1/chat/completions, embeddings)?

ava: route: /v1/chat/completions
Sample response headers they captured:
```
HTTP/1.1 429 Too Many Requests
Date: Tue, 02 Nov 2026 16:23:13 GMT
X-Request-Id: rq_A1b2c3d4
X-RateLimit-Limit: 120
X-RateLimit-Remaining: 0
Retry-After: 60
Content-Type: application/json
```
Body: {"error":{"message":"Request rate exceeded","type":"rate_limit"}}
They also included client logs showing a flood of ~2k requests in 30s (many retries).

ava: Their SDK is doing naive immediate retries on 429s which probably amplified the spike.

devin: Thanks. A few notes:
- Hosted APIs have a token-bucket-style burst credit system per API key and per route with a smoothing window. Short bursts are absorbed by credits; sustained high QPS consumes steady-state quota.
- If incoming traffic exceeds refill + credits, we 429 and set Retry-After. Retries from clients can create a retry storm and keep the key starved.

samir: Agreed. Quick triage checklist:
1) Confirm whether they hit a per-minute quota or a burst-window exhaustion (logs show Remaining=0 which points to immediate exhaustion).
2) Check server-side token-bucket metrics for org-987 (burst_credits, refill_rate, last_refill_ts).
3) See if requests were concentrated on one model variant/route (we can route to a cheaper variant during overload).

luis: I pulled the metrics for org-987 — burst_credits dropped to 0 at 16:23:11, refill_rate configured at 2 req/s, window 60s. They had a sudden spike of ~70 req/s for ~30s. That explains it. I temporarily bumped their per-minute quota by +500 requests for 1 hour and set an alert.

emma: Things we should tell the customer:
- Why it happened: that short spike exceeded their available burst credits; the platform returned 429 with Retry-After.
- Immediate mitigation: backoff and jitter on retries, client-side smoothing (token-bucket or leaky-bucket), and consider a short-term quota bump if needed.
- Medium term: tune refill/smoothing policy in Console or switch route to a model variant with higher throughput.

ava: Can we give them concrete client code for smoothing? They asked for a simple snippet.

samir: Here's a minimal client-side token bucket pseudo in JS — recommend adding full jitter to backoff:```
// simple token-bucket client smoothing
class TokenBucket {
  constructor(rate, capacity) {
    this.capacity = capacity; // tokens
    this.tokens = capacity;
    this.rate = rate; // tokens per second
    this.last = Date.now();
  }
  allow(n = 1) {
    const now = Date.now();
    const delta = (now - this.last) / 1000;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.capacity, this.tokens + delta * this.rate);
    this.last = now;
    if (this.tokens >= n) { this.tokens -= n; return true; }
    return false;
  }
}

// usage: rate=2 rps, capacity=60 (burst credits)
const bucket = new TokenBucket(2, 60);
async function sendWithSmoothing(req) {
  while (!bucket.allow()) await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 50));
  try {
    return await api.call(req);
  } catch (e) {
    if (e.status === 429) {
      // exponential backoff + full jitter
      const base = 100; // ms
      const max = 5000;
      let attempt = 0;
      while (attempt < 5) {
        const wait = Math.min(max, Math.random() * (2 ** attempt) * base);
        await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, wait));
        try { return await api.call(req); } catch (err) { if (err.status !== 429) throw err; }
        attempt++;
      }
      throw e;
    }
    throw e;
  }
}
```

samir: Also recommend client-side request coalescing for identical prompts and enabling prefix/KV cache when applicable.

luis: On our side: I enabled a temporary quota bump and created a runbook item. We should also consider lowering default burst credit drain on retries by adding a small server-side smoothing window (we've filed ENG-RUNTIME ticket #RT-4189).

emma: I’ll reply to the customer with the root cause, the temporary quota action, and the suggested client changes. Also include the JS snippet and link to our docs.

ava: Customer acknowledged — they’ll patch their SDK to add smoothing/backoff and test with a canary. They asked one question: how exactly do burst credits refill?

samir: Refills are continuous at the configured refill_rate (tokens/sec) up to capacity (burst credits). In Console you can view per-route capacity + refill. For hosted defaults we use conservative refill (to prioritize fairness).

emma: Follow-ups:
- I'll add a short FAQ blurb to support docs about "Why did I get a 429 during a short spike?" including the token-bucket explanation and example client smoothing snippet.
- Dev to ENG: please track RT-4189 for server-side smoothing-proof-of-concept.

runtime-bot: Incident note created: INC-20261102-429-spike-org-987. Assigned to samir, priority P3.

luis: Closing notes: reminded customer to send org usage patterns if they expect sustained bursts so we can advise a dedicated capacity or a higher steady-state quota. :thumbsup:

emma: Marking this thread resolved for support; re-open if the customer sees further 429s after SDK patch. :white_check_mark:
